## What to check

**Does every named test correspond to real acceptance criteria?**
Reconciliation trusts `<tests>` mechanically — it checks the name
exists and passes, not that the intent behind it was honored. So this
review is where vagueness gets caught instead of shipped. For each
`<test name="...">`, read `<acceptance>` and ask: if this test is
green, does a human actually know the thing the name claims? "Tests
error handling" with an acceptance of "errors are handled correctly" is
not acceptance criteria — it's a test that will pass while proving
nothing. Flag it by name and say what the acceptance should actually
claim.

**Does the test set cover the dependencies' failure_surface?** Walk
`{{ dependencies }}`'s failure_surface entries against `<tests>`: for
each declared failure mode a dependency can hand back, is there a test
exercising what this subcomponent does when it happens? A dependency
that can return three kinds of failure and gets exercised for one is a
gap that will only surface in production, exactly what the normative
`<tests>` block exists to catch before then.

**Is `<approach>` honest about what the dependencies and privapi
actually offer?** Cross-reference every claim in `<approach>` about
calling a dependency against `{{ dependencies }}`'s pubapi — if
`<approach>` describes calling a function, or relying on a behavior,
that pubapi doesn't document, that's an invented capability, not an
implementation detail; it will fail in a way this document gave no
warning of. Same check against `{{ subcomponent }}`'s own `<privapi>`
for how siblings are described reaching in.

**Boundary discipline.** Check that `<tests>` are described as testing
through an export module (this subcomponent's `<privapi>` or the
owning component's `<pubapi>`), not internals — an internal-only test
plan here is a maintenance cost the draft is committing the project to
without saying so.

**Store family property tests.** If this subcomponent is the
event-sourced store (or its owning component's techspec says so),
confirm `<tests>` includes `kind="property"` entries for
replay-determinism, idempotency, and upcaster round-trip. Their absence
here is blocking, not advisory — v5 §2.4 treats them as the family's
testing floor, not optional depth.

Return a `<review verdict="approve|revise">` with a `<summary>` and one
`<finding severity="blocking|advisory">` per issue — each `<location>`
naming the test name or `<approach>` claim at fault, each `<comment>`
saying what a passing version would contain.
